---
/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   / — the package, and the site's only offer.

   ONE OFFER, ONE PAGE. It used to be two rungs: a free analysis, and an hour
   on a call if they wanted it. The call is now half of what is promised at the
   top (docs/10-package-plan.md), which fixes the weakest joint in the funnel:
   an analysis that lands in an inbox and hopes. /reality-check and
   /book are redirects; there is no second offer to send anyone to.

   The path this route took: /exposure read as fear-mongering, and a URL is the
   first words a visitor sees. /report was accurate and dull, and it promised
   research when the thing being given away is advice. /brief redirects here.

   EVERY LEAD SEES THE CALENDAR. The scoring used to decide who was shown one.
   It no longer does: a booked meeting we cancel costs one email, and a
   qualified lead told to wait for one costs the lead. The score goes to the
   team in the alert, and the few that are not a fit are handled by hand.

   THE COPY SELLS THE ANALYSIS, NOT THE CITATIONS. The first version of this
   analysis led with sourced facts and a reader's verdict was "a dump of press
   release headlines, ChatGPT could have spit this out". They were right, and
   the fix was upstream. So this page promises what the brief now answers:
   where you stand, where you're strong, where you're exposed, what companies
   like you did and whether it worked, and what to weigh. Sourcing is the proof,
   not the pitch.

   The call is framed as course-correcting the brief rather than as a sales
   call, because that is what it honestly is: the document is built entirely
   from outside, the boundary section lists what we could not see, and the
   fastest way to fix that is an hour with the people who know.

   Positioning: written to the recommended position in docs/01 — design-led AI
   product studio with a care-operations wedge — and deliberately naming no
   vertical, so landing Decision #1 does not invalidate the page.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import IntakeForm from '../components/IntakeForm.astro';

const title = 'Free AI analysis and a 45-minute session — SeeSaw Labs';
const description =
  'A researched analysis of where AI pays off in your business, plus 45 minutes with the people who wrote it. We open with what we found rather than asking you to explain your business. Free, and yours to keep.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <!-- ------------------------------ hero ------------------------------ -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap hero__in">
      <p class="eyebrow">Free · Analysis + 45 minutes · Yours to keep</p>
      <h1 class="hero__h">
        AI just rewrote the roadmap.<br />
        <span class="hero__em">Let&rsquo;s build the one that ships.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="lede hero__lede">
        By the time we talk, we&rsquo;ll have already sized up where your
        industry is heading and where your business could go from here. The 45
        minutes is where that becomes a plan: what to build first, and what a
        team doing it with you would actually look like. A serious second
        opinion on the business, from people who&rsquo;d be the ones doing the
        work.
      </p>
      <div class="hero__cta">
        <a href="#request" class="btn">Get mine</a>
        <a href="#session" class="btn btn--ghost">What the 45 minutes is</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ---------------------------- the problem ------------------------- -->
  <section class="section section--sunk">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why this exists</p>
      <h2 class="h2">
        Plenty of people will tell you AI matters. Far fewer will tell you what
        to do about it in <em>your</em> business.
      </h2>
      <p class="body">
        You&rsquo;ve read the generic version already. The specific version
        takes real research. Before we write a word, we crawl your public
        surface the way a buyer reads it, sweep the web for what comparable
        companies have actually shipped and when, resolve each of those to a
        dated source we can hand you, pull live search-demand data for your
        category, and read the regulatory and funding movement around your
        sector. Then we do the part that isn&rsquo;t research: decide what
        we&rsquo;d build first, and why.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        That whole pipeline is something we built and run ourselves, which is
        why we can point it at your company before you&rsquo;ve paid us
        anything. It&rsquo;s also the most honest way to be judged &mdash; on
        the quality of the thinking, with the sources attached, rather than on
        a deck about our process.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- --------------------------- what's inside ------------------------ -->
  <section class="section" id="inside">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">What's in it</p>
        <h2 class="h2">What you actually get</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Six sections. The ideas and their numbers up front, the research they
          came from at the back, so any line of it can be checked.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="cards">
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card__h">Where we think you stand</h3>
          <p class="card__b">
            A short read on your position, built from the research: your own
            surface, your category, and the companies you get compared to.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card__h">Three or four ideas we&rsquo;d build</h3>
          <p class="card__b">
            Specific enough to disagree with, in the order we&rsquo;d do them.
            Not &ldquo;add AI to your operation&rdquo;.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card__h">What each one might be worth</h3>
          <p class="card__b">
            Arithmetic you can follow, so you can tell a serious idea from an
            expensive one before committing a quarter to it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card__h">The questions we&rsquo;d ask first</h3>
          <p class="card__b">
            The few answers that would change our recommendation. Useful even
            if you never reply &mdash; they are the questions worth asking
            internally.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card__h">What your competitors already shipped</h3>
          <p class="card__b">
            Their real AI moves, dated and sourced, with what each one suggests
            about where your category is heading. If nobody has moved yet,
            that is the finding.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card__h">Where your context sharpens it</h3>
          <p class="card__b">
            The specific facts that would take a recommendation from directional
            to costed. Bring those and the second version is a plan.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ------------------------- the session ---------------------------- -->
  <section class="section" id="session">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The other half</p>
        <h2 class="h2">45 minutes that start where a first call usually ends</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          A normal discovery call spends its first twenty minutes on background,
          and its last five on next steps. We do the background beforehand, in
          writing, so the time goes on the decisions.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="steps">
        <li class="step">
          <h3 class="step__h">We open with what we found</h3>
          <p class="step__b">
            Our read of your business, out loud, in the first five minutes. You
            tell us where it&rsquo;s wrong. Nothing sharpens a recommendation
            faster than the person who knows the operation correcting it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <h3 class="step__h">We put our questions to you</h3>
          <p class="step__b">
            The handful whose answers would change what we&rsquo;d recommend:
            your volumes, your cycle times, which manual step is quietly costing
            the most. The analysis names them, so you see the agenda before you
            agree to the call.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <h3 class="step__h">We tell you what we&rsquo;ve watched fail</h3>
          <p class="step__b">
            The part research can&rsquo;t give you. Nobody publishes the pilot
            that died in security review, or the model that worked and shipped
            to nobody. We build this in healthcare operations, including the
            platform that cut medication approvals 5x at Hospice Pharmacy
            Solutions, and we&rsquo;ve seen the expensive dead ends up close.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <h3 class="step__h">You leave with a sequence, not a list</h3>
          <p class="step__b">
            What to do first, what to park, and what has to be true before the
            next thing starts. Ordered against your capacity rather than our
            enthusiasm.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="sessionnote">
        You&rsquo;ll be talking to the team that would lead the work, not a
        salesperson. And if there&rsquo;s no fit, we say so inside the 45
        minutes rather than in a follow-up sequence.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ------------------------ the honesty promise --------------------- -->
  <section class="section section--sunk">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">How we work</p>
        <h2 class="h2">The same discipline we&rsquo;d bring to the build</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="two">
        <div>
          <p class="body">
            Every number in your brief either links to the source we found it
            in, or is an assumption we state with the reasoning attached. Where
            only you can know the figure, it stays a
            visible <span class="blankdemo">blank</span> rather than a
            confident guess. That is enforced in the pipeline, not in a style
            guide: a claim we cannot trace never reaches you.
          </p>
          <p class="body">
            It is how we work on paid engagements too. Reasoning in the open,
            assumptions labelled, so the people who would have to live with a
            decision can test it rather than take it on trust. A
            recommendation you cannot inspect is one you cannot act on.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="body">
            The brief is deliberately the outside view. It sees your public
            surface, your category, and what your competitors have shipped. It
            does not see your volumes, your cycle times, or which manual step
            is quietly costing you the most.
          </p>
          <p class="body">
            That is what the hour is for. You bring the operating detail, we
            bring a version of this document that is costed and sequenced.
            Most of our engagements begin at exactly that point: a shared view
            of the work, arrived at together, before anyone signs anything.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="samplelink">
        <!-- Points at the brief we ran on ourselves. /sample is the Reality
             Check assessment, a different document, and linking there from here
             showed a visitor the structure of a deliverable nobody asked for. -->
        <a href="/sample-brief">Read the one we ran on ourselves &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ------------------------------ request ---------------------------- -->
  <section class="section" id="request">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Four fields, no calendar</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Point us at the right things</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The research goes wherever you send it. Two answers steer all of it:
          how you&rsquo;d describe the business in one line, and who you
          actually count as a competitor. Get those right and the brief is
          about your market rather than your industry.
        </p>
      </div>
      <IntakeForm />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ------------------------------- close ---------------------------- -->
  <section class="section section--deep">
    <div class="wrap-narrow close">
      <p class="eyebrow">What it costs</p>
      <h2 class="close__h">Nothing, and we mean the whole thing.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        The analysis and the 45 minutes are both free, and both yours to keep.
        We do it because it is the most honest way to be judged: on the quality
        of the thinking, with the sources attached. If we go on to build
        together, engagements start at $50k. If we don&rsquo;t, you still have
        a researched view of where AI pays off in your business.
      </p>
      <div class="close__ctas">
        <a href="#request" class="btn btn--on-deep">Get mine</a>
        <a href="/sample-brief" class="close__more">Read the one we ran on ourselves &rarr;</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    /* Local to this page. Shared primitives live in styles/tokens.css. */
    .hero { padding-block: clamp(var(--sp-16), 10vw, var(--sp-24)) var(--sp-16); }
    .hero__in { display: grid; gap: var(--sp-5); }
    .hero__h {
      font-size: clamp(var(--text-4xl), 6.5vw, var(--text-6xl));
      font-weight: 600;
      line-height: var(--lh-tight);
      letter-spacing: var(--ls-tight);
      margin: 0;
      text-wrap: balance;
    }
    .hero__em { color: var(--accent); }
    .hero__lede { max-width: 56ch; margin: 0; }
    .hero__cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--sp-3); margin-top: var(--sp-2); }
    .hero__fine { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--muted); margin: 0; }

    .h2 {
      font-size: clamp(var(--text-2xl), 3.6vw, var(--text-4xl));
      font-weight: 600;
      line-height: var(--lh-tight);
      letter-spacing: var(--ls-tight);
      margin: var(--sp-3) 0 var(--sp-5);
      text-wrap: balance;
    }
    .h2 em { font-style: italic; color: var(--accent); }
    .section--deep .h2 { color: #fff; }

    .head { max-width: 60ch; margin-bottom: var(--sp-10); }
    .head .lede { margin: 0; }

    .body {
      font-size: var(--text-lg);
      line-height: var(--lh-relaxed);
      color: var(--ink-2);
      max-width: 60ch;
      margin: 0 0 var(--sp-5);
    }
    .body:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

    .cards {
      list-style: none;
      counter-reset: card;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      display: grid;
      gap: var(--sp-5);
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(17rem, 1fr));
    }
    .card {
      counter-increment: card;
      position: relative;
      background: var(--surface);
      border: 1px solid var(--rule);
      border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
      padding: var(--sp-6);
    }
    .card::before {
      content: counter(card, decimal-leading-zero);
      font-family: var(--font-accent);
      font-size: var(--text-sm);
      color: var(--accent);
      letter-spacing: var(--ls-wide);
    }
    .card__h {
      font-size: var(--text-xl);
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: var(--ls-tight);
      line-height: var(--lh-snug);
      margin: var(--sp-2) 0 var(--sp-3);
    }
    .card__b { font-size: var(--text-md); line-height: var(--lh-relaxed); color: var(--ink-2); margin: 0; }

    .two { display: grid; gap: var(--sp-8); }
    @media (min-width: 48rem) {
      .two { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--sp-10); }
    }

    /* Mirrors how a declared blank renders inside the brief itself. */
    .blankdemo {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: 0.9em;
      color: var(--accent-deep);
      background: var(--accent-soft);
      border-bottom: 2px solid var(--accent);
      padding: 0.05rem 0.3rem;
      border-radius: var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0 0;
      white-space: nowrap;
    }

    .samplelink { margin: var(--sp-10) 0 0; font-size: var(--text-lg); }
    .samplelink a { color: var(--accent-deep); font-weight: 500; }

    /* The session steps: numbered, because the order is the point. */
    ol.steps {
      list-style: none;
      counter-reset: st;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      display: grid;
      gap: var(--sp-8);
      max-width: var(--page-narrow);
    }
    .step { counter-increment: st; display: grid; gap: var(--sp-2); position: relative; padding-left: var(--sp-10); }
    .step::before {
      content: counter(st);
      position: absolute;
      left: 0;
      top: 0;
      font-family: var(--font-accent);
      font-size: var(--text-lg);
      line-height: 1.1;
      color: var(--accent);
    }
    .step__h { font-size: var(--text-xl); font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: var(--ls-tight); margin: 0; }
    .step__b { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-2); line-height: var(--lh-body); max-width: 62ch; }
    .sessionnote {
      margin: var(--sp-10) 0 0;
      padding-top: var(--sp-6);
      border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
      color: var(--ink-2);
      max-width: 62ch;
    }

    .close { display: grid; gap: var(--sp-5); justify-items: start; }
    .close__ctas { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--sp-5); align-items: center; }
    .close__more { color: inherit; opacity: .82; font-size: var(--text-base); }
    .close__h {
      font-size: clamp(var(--text-3xl), 5vw, var(--text-5xl));
      font-weight: 600;
      line-height: var(--lh-tight);
      letter-spacing: var(--ls-tight);
      margin: 0;
      color: #fff;
      text-wrap: balance;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
